{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011oFwcJSfqwqz7Ki1oRLeZc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have found clear examples of the flag parsing patterns used in this repo. Let me provide you with a comprehensive analysis.\n\n## Flag Parsing Patterns in go-unix-utils\n\nThis repo uses **manual flag parsing** (not the standard Go `flag` package). Here's what I found across 3 utilities:\n\n### Pattern Overview\n\nAll utilities follow the same consistent manual parsing architecture:\n\n1. **Main parsing loop** in `parseArgs()` that distinguishes flag types\n2. **Separate handlers** for long flags (`--flag`) and short flags (`-f`)\n3. **Manual value extraction** for flags that take arguments\n\n---\n\n### **1. cmd/split — Most Complex (Full-Featured)**\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5655/cmd/split/main.go`\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `parseArgs()` dispatches to `parseLongFlag()` or `parseShortFlags()` (lines 67-99)\n- Returns count of consumed arguments to advance the loop\n\n**Long flags (lines 115-167):**\n```\n--lines / --lines=VALUE\n--bytes / --bytes=VALUE\n--number / --number=VALUE\n--numeric-suffixes (boolean)\n--additional-suffix / --additional-suffix=VALUE\n--filter / --filter=VALUE\n```\n\nUses `strings.HasPrefix(flag, \"--name=\")` to detect equals-style values, otherwise takes next arg from remaining slice.\n\n**Short flags (lines 169-192):**\n- Boolean flags (like `-d` for numeric suffixes)\n- Flags with values (like `-l NUMBER`, `-b BYTES`, `-a SUFFIX_LEN`)\n- Supports both `-lVALUE` (attached) and `-l VALUE` (separate arg)\n- Uses `isBoolFlag()` to distinguish; non-bool flags use a `shortFlagSetter` function map\n\n**Key extraction function (lines 225-233):**\n```go\nfunc extractFlagValue(rest string, remaining []string, consumed int, flag byte) (string, int, error) {\n    if rest != \"\" {\n        return rest, 0, nil  // -lVALUE\n    }\n    if len(remaining) > consumed {\n        return remaining[consumed], 1, nil  // -l VALUE\n    }\n    return \"\", 0, fmt.Errorf(\"option requires an argument -- '%c'\", flag)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### **2. cmd/truncate — Medium Complexity**\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5655/cmd/truncate/main.go`\n\n**Long flags (lines 255-293):**\n- `--size=VALUE` (with equals), `--size VALUE` (separate)\n- `--reference=VALUE`, `--reference VALUE`\n- Boolean: `--no-create`, `--io-blocks`, `--help`, `--version`\n\nUses direct string prefix checks for equals-style:\n```go\nif strings.HasPrefix(flag, \"--size=\") {\n    opts.sizeStr = flag[len(\"--size=\"):]\n    opts.sizeSet = true\n    return 0, nil\n}\nif flag == \"--size\" {\n    if len(rest) == 0 {\n        return 0, fmt.Errorf(\"option '%s' requires an argument\", flag)\n    }\n    opts.sizeStr = rest[0]\n    opts.sizeSet = true\n    return 1, nil\n}\n```\n\n**Short flags (lines 295-330):**\n- `-c` (boolean)\n- `-o` (boolean)\n- `-s VALUE` (takes value, allows `-sVALUE` or `-s VALUE`)\n- `-r VALUE` (takes value, allows `-rVALUE` or `-r VALUE`)\n\nInline value extraction:\n```go\ncase 's':\n    opts.sizeSet = true\n    remaining := flags[i+1:]\n    if remaining != \"\" {\n        opts.sizeStr = remaining  // -sVALUE\n        return 0, nil\n    }\n    if len(rest) == 0 {\n        return 0, fmt.Errorf(\"option requires an argument -- 's'\")\n    }\n    opts.sizeStr = rest[0]  // -s VALUE\n    return 1, nil\n```\n\n---\n\n### **3. cmd/expand — Simplest (Minimal Flags)**\n\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5655/cmd/expand/main.go`\n\n**Only flag:** `-t` or `-tVALUE` for tab stops (lines 56-92)\n\n```go\nif a == \"-t\" || strings.HasPrefix(a, \"-t\") {\n    var val string\n    if a == \"-t\" {\n        if i+1 >= len(args) {\n            return tabStops{}, nil, fmt.Errorf(\"option requires an argument -- 't'\")\n        }\n        i++\n        val = args[i]\n    } else {\n        val = a[2:]  // Everything after -t\n    }\n    rawStops = append(rawStops, val)\n    continue\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Key Patterns & Decisions\n\n| Aspect | Pattern |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Package** | Manual parsing (no `flag` package) |\n| **Long flags** | Both `--flag=value` and `--flag value` supported |\n| **Short flags** | Both `-fvalue` and `-f value` supported; boolean flags work in sequences like `-abc` |\n| **Compound shorts** | Yes — multiple booleans can combine (`-abc` for three boolean flags), but stops at first value-taking flag |\n| **Flag separator** | Standard `--` support |\n| **Return value** | Functions return count of consumed arguments (`n`) to advance loop correctly |\n| **Value functions** | Dedicated setter functions (`setLineMode`, `setBytesMode`, etc.) in split; inline switches in truncate |\n| **Error handling** | Custom error messages matching GNU tool format |\n\n---\n\n## Summary for Your Implementation\n\nFor your utility, follow the **split pattern** (most complete) or **truncate pattern** (simpler but still full-featured):\n\n1. Loop through args with index `i`\n2. Dispatch to `parseLongFlag()` and `parseShortFlags()` based on prefix\n3. Return consumed count to advance `i` correctly\n4. Handle both `--flag=val` and `--flag val` in long flags\n5. Handle both `-fval` and `-f val` in short flags\n6. For boolean short flags, support chaining (`-abc`)\n7. Once a short flag takes a value, return immediately (can't chain after)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"165afc1e-4c0e-454d-909a-66ac00073bd4","uuid":"f5532b95-94ab-44bf-862b-d28f4f6aecd1","timestamp":"2026-05-29T17:04:05.982Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5655, I need to understand how other cmd/ utilities parse CLI flags. Look at 2-3 cmd/ directories that have flag parsing (e.g. 