{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HfLdxGKwsLnbEyiXYPFHnR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's still a difference. Let me check the gap size. Our output has 4-space gaps in some lines, gptx has 3-space gaps.\n\nFor keyword \"b\":\nOur: \"                                  a    b c\"\ngptx: \"                                   a   b c\"\n\nLet me count characters more carefully.\n\nOur output for keyword \"b\":\nLength of \"                                  a    b c\"\n\nLet me count spaces before \"a\":\n\"                                  a\" — how many spaces? Let me count: 34 spaces + \"a\" = 35.\n\n\nThen \"    b c\" = 4 spaces + \"b c\" = 7.\nTotal = 35 + 7 = 42.\n\ngptx output for keyword \"b\":\n\"                                   a   b c\"\n35 spaces + \"a\" + 3 spaces + \"b c\" = 35 + 1 + 3 + 3 = 42.\n\nBoth are 42 characters, but the gap differs—ours uses 4 spaces between \"a\" and \"b\", while gptx uses 3. Let me trace through the code logic for keyword \"b\" in \"a b c\" to see where this discrepancy comes from.\n\nWith left = \"a \" (including the trailing space), half = 36, and gapSize = 3, the padding calculation gives 36 - 2 = 34 spaces before \"a\", then 3 spaces before \"b c\". But gptx appears to treat left as just \"a\" without the trailing space, resulting in 35 spaces of padding instead. The difference hinges on whether the text before the keyword includes its trailing space or not. I see the pattern now — gptx is stripping trailing whitespace from the left context before calculating padding. For \"b\", the left side should be \"a \" but it's being trimmed to \"a\", which changes the padding calculation from 36 to 35. Same thing happens with \"c\" where \"a b \" becomes \"a b\". I need to apply that same trimming logic to match the output. I'm realizing the issue might be how gptx handles the text before the keyword — it could be storing everything up to the end