{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YYEqCxhuMNwRfC5byXUnWy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"For keyword \"b\" with double spaces, input \"a  b  c\":\n- \"b\" is at position 3 (0-indexed: \"a  b\" = positions 0,1,2,3)\n\nWait: \"a  b  c\"\nPosition 0: 'a'\nPosition 1: ' '\nPosition 2: ' '\nPosition 3: 'b'\nPosition 4: ' '\nPosition 5: ' '\nPosition 6: 'c'\n\nSo before \"b\" (position 3) = \"a  \" (3 chars, with 2 trailing spaces).\n\n\nThe part after \"b\" is \"b  c\" (4 chars). When gptx formats this with a 36-character width, it strips the trailing spaces from the left side, showing just \"a\", then pads to 35 spaces, adds the 3-character gap, and appends the right portion—totaling 43 characters. But our output keeps those trailing spaces in the left part, so we're calculating the padding differently.\n\nI need to trim trailing spaces from the left context to match gptx's behavior. I'm wondering if gptx only trims spaces or all whitespace characters, but I'll start with just trimming spaces using a simple string operation.\n\nLooking at the right context, it seems gptx preserves leading spaces and internal spacing, so I only need to handle the left side. Let me implement this fix.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1707,"cache_read_input_tokens":70066,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1707},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"165afc1e-4c0e-454d-909a-66ac00073bd4","uuid":"364618e8-f006-431c-850b-83741e3666b0"}
